From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 19:21:24 -0400
Subject: colours: sand light, blue-to-plum dark gradient

Light is #C8B377 sand. Dark is a #21263A to #31243A top-left to bottom-right
gradient painted once by SteddingWindowBackground on BrowserView, with the
sidebar, top container and mat made invisible over it so the chrome reads as
one surface. Colour mixers hand out single colours, so the gradient cannot
come from a mixer; the invisible (not removed) backgrounds are because the
layout dereferences them.

Hovered and selected tab rows tint with their own text colour at a low
alpha instead of Material's blue header hover and select.

The active Space's colour tints the window ground (30% in dark, 22% in
light) the way Arc tints its sidebar; the gradient painter reads the tinted
frame colour back from the provider so ground and chrome agree. The new tab
page keeps a neutral ground, and there is no hairline between toolbar and
content.

Why: the operator picked the colours; one ground with no seams is what makes
the chrome feel like one object.
Removable when: Stedding grows real theme settings, where these become the
defaults rather than the only choice.
